# AI working state

Globally-useful state only. Detail lives in `state/<track>.md` — see the
index below. Read this file first every session; read a track's state file
only when you are touching that track.

## Latest

Profiled by label, and it paid for itself. A mixed-codec run — two
generators, 250 connections each on JSON and msgpack, 10% at 2/s into one
room, the same 500-connection shape as every steady run in
`state/loadgen.md` — says `go tool pprof -tags`: 97.16% `channel-pump`,
1.42% `conn`, the rest the runtime, and 50.35% JSON against 48.23% msgpack.
Two findings. **msgpack costs the server 4.2% less CPU than JSON for
identical delivered work**, three quarters of it `write(2)` moving fewer
bytes — the first server-side number for a codec choice that had only ever
been argued from frame size, and something no call graph can produce because
both codecs run the same functions. And **the entire read path is 1.42% of
CPU**, which retires the standing "the next slice is the read pump's
per-read `context.WithTimeout`" note: the ceiling on it is under 1.4%.
Nothing regressed (`Syscall6` 71.87% flat against 72.3% recorded), the write
path still allocates nothing, and the goroutine profile is a census of the
connection model — 1500 goroutines, 500 each of `conn`, `priv-pump` and
`channel-pump`. One trap: **labels are in the CPU and goroutine profiles and
not the allocation profile**. Detail in `state/loadgen.md`.

## State file index

- `state/broadcast.md` — `internal/broadcast`, the fan-out primitive and
  its comparative benchmark harness.
- `state/server.md` — config, wire codecs, extension points, and the
  connection handling built on the fan-out.
- `state/loadgen.md` — `internal/loadgen` and `cmd/loadgen`, the
  out-of-process load generator and what it has measured.
- `state/ci.md` — the Woodpecker pipeline and the Makefile targets a
  clone needs.

